1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,527
One small change. Many steps.

2
00:00:03,077 --> 00:00:06,533
CloudCart wants free shipping on orders over fifty dollars.

3
00:00:07,083 --> 00:00:11,840
It sounds simple. But a change passes through many hands before customers see it.

4
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:15,291
Tracing it shows how the whole life cycle connects.

5
00:00:15,841 --> 00:00:18,849
Today you follow one change, from idea to feedback.

6
00:00:20,899 --> 00:00:23,054
Here is the journey, in seven steps.

7
00:00:23,604 --> 00:00:25,993
First, a requirement says what is needed.

8
00:00:26,543 --> 00:00:28,015
Then code makes it happen.

9
00:00:28,565 --> 00:00:29,930
Reviewers check the code.

10
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:31,739
Tests prove it works.

11
00:00:32,289 --> 00:00:36,193
A release packages the approved version, and deployment puts it live.

12
00:00:36,743 --> 00:00:38,258
Feedback shows how it went.

13
00:00:38,808 --> 00:00:40,258
Then the loop starts again.

14
00:00:42,308 --> 00:00:43,972
Step one: the requirement.

15
00:00:44,522 --> 00:00:47,679
Product writes a ticket: C A R T, one forty-two.

16
00:00:48,229 --> 00:00:50,213
It includes acceptance criteria.

17
00:00:50,763 --> 00:00:54,411
Given a cart of fifty dollars or more, shipping shows zero dollars.

18
00:00:54,961 --> 00:00:58,609
Acceptance criteria turn an idea into something testable.

19
00:01:00,659 --> 00:01:01,875
Step two: the code.

20
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:05,945
A developer creates a branch, a separate copy of the main code.

21
00:01:06,495 --> 00:01:09,311
They change the shipping rule, and write a unit test.

22
00:01:09,861 --> 00:01:13,232
Each saved change is a commit, with its own identifier.

23
00:01:13,782 --> 00:01:16,662
In this example, the commit links back to the ticket.

24
00:01:18,712 --> 00:01:19,949
Step three: review.

25
00:01:20,499 --> 00:01:22,547
The developer opens a pull request.

26
00:01:23,097 --> 00:01:25,913
A teammate reads the change, and asks questions.

27
00:01:26,463 --> 00:01:29,087
Review catches mistakes, and shares knowledge.

28
00:01:29,637 --> 00:01:33,157
Only after approval does the change merge into the main code.

29
00:01:35,207 --> 00:01:36,508
Step four: test.

30
00:01:37,058 --> 00:01:40,237
When the change merges, a pipeline starts automatically.

31
00:01:40,787 --> 00:01:44,008
It builds the application, and runs the automated tests.

32
00:01:44,558 --> 00:01:47,204
In this example, build three eighteen passes.

33
00:01:47,754 --> 00:01:50,762
QA also checks the change in a test environment.

34
00:01:51,312 --> 00:01:54,384
If a test fails, the change goes back to the developer.

35
00:01:56,434 --> 00:01:57,799
Step five: release.

36
00:01:58,349 --> 00:02:02,040
The approved build gets a version number: one point four point three.

37
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:04,595
Release notes explain what changed.

38
00:02:05,145 --> 00:02:09,049
The version number lets everyone say exactly which code is running.

39
00:02:11,099 --> 00:02:12,614
Step six: deployment.

40
00:02:13,164 --> 00:02:16,940
The release goes to a test environment first, then to production.

41
00:02:17,490 --> 00:02:20,455
The deployment is recorded: what, when, and by whom.

42
00:02:21,005 --> 00:02:24,376
A safe deployment can be repeated, and reversed if needed.

43
00:02:24,926 --> 00:02:27,934
Environments and rollback get their own lesson next.

44
00:02:29,984 --> 00:02:31,477
Step seven: feedback.

45
00:02:32,027 --> 00:02:34,673
Monitoring shows errors, speed, and traffic.

46
00:02:35,223 --> 00:02:38,572
Customers react: orders, comments, and support tickets.

47
00:02:39,122 --> 00:02:42,045
Did free shipping increase orders? Did anything break?

48
00:02:42,595 --> 00:02:45,965
Feedback becomes the next requirement, and the loop continues.

49
00:02:48,015 --> 00:02:50,084
Notice that every step left a trail.

50
00:02:50,634 --> 00:02:51,214
The ticket,

51
00:02:51,214 --> 00:02:51,794
the commit,

52
00:02:51,794 --> 00:02:52,690
the pull request,

53
00:02:52,690 --> 00:02:53,218
the build,

54
00:02:53,218 --> 00:02:53,850
the release,

55
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:55,221
and the deployment record.

56
00:02:55,771 --> 00:02:57,392
This is called traceability.

57
00:02:57,942 --> 00:03:01,441
If something breaks, you can follow the trail backwards to the cause.

58
00:03:01,991 --> 00:03:04,295
Good teams make every step leave evidence.

59
00:03:06,345 --> 00:03:09,438
Now suppose customers report a wrong shipping price.

60
00:03:09,988 --> 00:03:13,252
Start from the symptom, and ask: which version is running?

61
00:03:13,802 --> 00:03:16,405
That points to the release, and the build behind it.

62
00:03:16,955 --> 00:03:19,131
Then to the pull request, and its review.

63
00:03:19,681 --> 00:03:22,198
And finally to the ticket, to see what was asked.

64
00:03:22,748 --> 00:03:25,778
Tracing backwards turns a mystery into a short search.

65
00:03:27,828 --> 00:03:31,049
Where does the problem start? Match each symptom to a step.

66
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:35,397
Nobody agrees what free shipping means? The requirement was unclear.

67
00:03:35,947 --> 00:03:38,827
The change broke another page? The tests missed it.

68
00:03:39,377 --> 00:03:43,195
A bug went unnoticed for a week? Monitoring and feedback were weak.

69
00:03:43,745 --> 00:03:48,332
Nobody knows which version is live? The release and deployment records are missing.

70
00:03:48,882 --> 00:03:52,146
Problems often show up late, but they usually start early.

71
00:03:54,196 --> 00:03:56,052
Watch out for four common myths.

72
00:03:56,602 --> 00:03:59,784
Myth one: a change is finished when the code is written.

73
00:03:59,784 --> 00:04:00,239
In fact,

74
00:04:00,239 --> 00:04:02,682
it is finished when it works for customers.

75
00:04:03,232 --> 00:04:07,776
Myth two: testing is one step at the end. In fact, testing happens throughout.

76
00:04:08,326 --> 00:04:11,910
Myth three: feedback is optional. Without it, the team is guessing.

77
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:17,303
Myth four: small changes do not need the full path. Small changes break things too.

78
00:04:17,853 --> 00:04:19,858
Follow the whole path, every time.

79
00:04:21,908 --> 00:04:22,783
Quick check.

80
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:25,082
Name the seven steps, in order.

81
00:04:25,632 --> 00:04:27,573
What are acceptance criteria for?

82
00:04:28,123 --> 00:04:29,446
What is traceability?

83
00:04:29,996 --> 00:04:33,836
Why is feedback the last step, and also the first of the next change?

84
00:04:34,386 --> 00:04:38,439
Objective covered: MC01-O06.

85
00:04:38,989 --> 00:04:42,509
Pause the video, and write the seven steps for a change of your own.

86
00:04:55,059 --> 00:04:57,641
Next lesson: environments and releases.

87
00:04:58,191 --> 00:05:00,068
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
